  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a magnetic tunnel junction memory element integrated on a conducting strip, illustrating separate write and read paths. A cylindrical stack sits on the strip and contains, from bottom to top, an antiferromagnetic layer labeled IrMn, a CoFeB layer, an MgO tunnel barrier, and another CoFeB layer. Arrows inside the layers indicate magnetic orientations: the upper CoFeB appears magnetized in one in-plane direction, while the lower CoFeB points oppositely, and the IrMn layer is associated with exchange bias or spin structure at the base.\n\nA horizontal current labeled \\(I_{write}\\) runs through the underlying strip rather than directly through the pillar, suggesting write operation by an in-plane current in the heavy-metal or electrode layer. A separate vertical current labeled \\(I_{read}\\) passes through the cylindrical stack, indicating conventional tunnel-junction readout across the MgO barrier. Overall, the image conveys a three-terminal spintronic device concept in which writing and reading are electrically separated: the bottom current is used to switch or influence the magnetic state, and the perpendicular current senses the resistance state of the junction.",
